• Section 1 (Five Widespread Problems & Five Root Causes in Current Damage Control Plans, pp. 3–6):
  — Five Problems (1.1): (1) **Copying plans behind closed doors (闭门造车)** from other ships with minor tweaks to cope with superior inspections, without on-site compartment checks; (2) **Inadequate risk analysis & artificial scenarios** where missile/torpedo breach sizes and locations are artificially set to easy levels rather than worst-case combat damage; (3) **Arbitrary decision-making** lacking theoretical analysis of damage progression; (4) **Unfocused organization & missing teams**—unclear sequencing when fire, flooding, and casualties occur simultaneously (e.g., whether to plug a leak or fight a fire first), and omitting firefighting groups, anti-sinking groups (抗沉组), rescue groups, assessment groups, or training directing groups (导调组); and (5) **Formalistic drills (流于形式)** and failing to update plans when crews rotate, equipment is modified, or ships age.
  — Five Root Causes (1.2): (1) Low officer theoretical understanding of hull compartments and weapon effects; (2) **A ‘luck mentality’ (侥幸心理) among Commanding Officers**, leaving the **Chief Mechanical/Electrical Officer (机电长)** to write the entire ship’s plan alone without other level commanders; (3) Weak drafting work with no drafting group, scientific division of labor, or tabletop simulations (推演); (4) Overly simplistic skills training that ignores ‘brief, flat, and rapid’ (短、平、快) total-factor coordination, personnel escape (人员逃生), and abandoning ship; and (5) **Headquarters departments (机关业务部门) that ‘prioritize delegation and under-emphasize implementation’ (重部署轻落实)**, lack whole-of-process accountability, and ‘just walk around contradictions.’

• Section 2 (Seven-Step Quantitative Methodology for Preparing Damage Control Plans, pp. 6–14):
  (1) Form a Captain-led drafting group including the XO, department heads, zone managers, and compartment/combat-post leaders;
  (2) Collate compartment and system layouts (fore/aft, port/starboard, above/below) into compartment **firefighting cards (灭火卡)** and **anti-sinking cards (抗沉卡)**;
  (3) Draft risk assessment reports taking main watertight compartments below/across the waterline as the smallest anti-sinking unit;
  (4) **Quantify weapon blast and flooding data**: Underwater weapons (torpedoes, mines, depth charges) produce a hull damage radius of **≥5 meters**, while guided missiles (seeker, forward, warhead, engine, and rear sections) produce a damage radius of **≥2 meters**. Using **Formula 1 ($t = \frac{klbz}{\mu s \sqrt{2gH}}$)** for an aft engine room ($l=16\text{ m}, b=8.8\text{ m}, z=3\text{ m}, k=0.85, H=3\text{ m}$), **Table 1** proves why rapid leak-plugging is decisive: an unplugged 200-cm breach ($3.14\text{ m}^2$) floods in **43 seconds**, a 60-cm breach ($0.283\text{ m}^2$) in **473s**, a 30-cm breach ($0.07\text{ m}^2$) in **1,914s (~32 min)**, and a 10-cm breach ($0.008\text{ m}^2$) in **16,750s (~4.65 hrs)**;
  (5–7) Follow the verification flowchart (Fig. 1), specify transferable command interfaces, achieve the **‘Four Goals’ (四化: Simple [简化], Realistic [实化], Streamlined [流程化], Illustrated [图解化])** using tables and flowcharts, and run closed-loop drills (‘planning – drilling – revising – drilling’) with smoke generators and leak-plugging simulators.

• Section 3 (Verified Combat Effectiveness Gains, p. 14): Revising and drilling a ship class’s damage control plan under this methodology improved plan objectivity and practicability by **80%**, increased plan focus by **85%**, raised all commanders’ damage-control theory scores above **95 points**, and lifted command ability scores to **≥90 points**.
